<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fergus of Galloway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1161</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fergus of Galloway was the first known Lord of Galloway in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, rising to power in the early 12th century and styled king in some sources. He married an illegitimate daughter of Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, founded religious houses such as Soulseat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whithorn">Whithorn</a> Priory, and revived the bishopric of Whithorn. After a failed revolt against Malcolm IV he retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a> in 1160.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fergus-of-galloway.jpg" alt="Fergus of Galloway" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RomanDeFergus.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
